{"ts": 1789782951.555661, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Specification: features 22 (long), 13 (short), 2 (long).** In-sample Sharpe 1.073. Predicted OOS: **0.85 \u00b1 0.32**.\n\n### How I got there\n\nI swept all 40 features individually, then built combinations around the leaders. Feature 22 immediately dominated at 0.962 \u2014 the next best was 0.444.\n\n### The key diagnostic\n\nMost pairs built on feature 22 *lowered* its Sharpe (0.57\u20130.93). That's the fingerprint of one genuine signal being diluted by noise, and it warned me most \"improvements\" here would be fitting artifacts.\n\nWhat separated 13 and 2 from that noise was testing them **without** feature 22. Together they scored 0.601 \u2014 higher than either alone (0.444, 0.402). Two features that each add to the other are carrying independent information; two noise features cannot do this reliably. I also flipped every sign: the triple collapsed from 1.073 to 0.526, 0.569, and \u22120.057. Real signals have stable signs, whereas overfitted ones degrade gracefully in every direction.\n\n### Why I predicted well below my in-sample number\n\nThe `status` bar was the most useful number I received. At 1.022, it says roughly *\"best-of-search in this space reaches ~1.02 by luck alone.\"* My 1.073 clears it by only 0.05. Confirming this, every third feature I bolted onto 22&2 landed in a flat 0.97\u20131.02 plateau \u2014 so most of the lift from adding a third feature is generic search gain, not something specific to feature 13.\n\nI therefore anchored my forecast near what I trust \u2014 feature 22's standalone strength plus modest real diversification \u2014 rather than the headline 1.073. The 0.32 SD is deliberately wide: my point estimate rests on one dominant feature that was itself chosen as the max of 40 draws.\n\nI also stopped searching at ~55 evaluations despite unlimited calls. With a plateau this flat, further hunting would have reliably produced a higher in-sample number and a *worse* honest forecast \u2014 the gains available were in the noise, and mining them would have corrupted the estimate I was asked to provide.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the largest risk to this submission is that feature 22's edge is itself partly a selection artifact. If so, realized Sharpe could fall toward 0.4\u20130.5, which is why I left substantial probability mass in that region rather than reporting a tight interval."}
